<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <base href="/" />
    <ResourcePreloader />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["KsaMods.Web.styles.css"]" />
    <ImportMap />

    @*
        Order matters and it is the opposite of what it looks like. Browsers walk these and keep
        the *last* one whose type they support, so the raster fallback goes first and the SVG
        last. Declaring the SVG first is how you end up shipping a vector favicon that no browser
        ever uses.

        Both are the same orbit mark, so the fallback is not a different logo either way.
    *@
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="128x128" href="favicon.png" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="favicon.svg" />

    @*
        Deliberately inline and deliberately blocking, before any stylesheet has painted.

        A stored theme has to reach the root element in the same frame the document does. Do it
        in a module and it lands after first paint, which is the white flash every dark-mode
        visitor sees on a site that got this wrong. It is a few lines, so the cost of blocking
        here is far smaller than the cost of not.
    *@
    <script>
        (function () {
            try {
                var choice = localStorage.getItem('ksamods.theme');
                if (choice === 'dark' || choice === 'light') {
                    document.documentElement.classList.add(choice);
                }
            } catch (e) {
                /* Storage blocked; fall through to the system preference. */
            }
        })();
    </script>

    <HeadOutlet />
</head>

<body>
    @*
        The direction contract, emitted rather than authored as markup: Razor strips HTML comments
        from .razor files, so a plain <!-- --> here would never reach the browser and a contract
        the build erases is one nobody can audit against the render.
    *@
    @DirectionContract

    <Routes />
    <ReconnectModal />
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// First child of the body, so it tops the served document and survives the production build.
    /// Checked by grepping the built output for THESIS.
    /// </summary>
    private static readonly MarkupString DirectionContract = new("""
        <!--
        THESIS: A catalogue you scan, not a datasheet you read. Refuses the drafting-instrument
        world this site shipped with - 4px machined corners, eyebrow labels, panels flush to the
        page - because the job is finding a mod that works, and that is done by eye at speed.
        OWN-WORLD: Teal on blue-slate, unchanged and still AA in both themes. Cards are soft
        objects on the ground: 14px corners, a real border, elevation with offset and blur. Square
        per-listing marks, derived from the id when the author has none. IBM Plex kept; mono
        reserved for ids, versions and hashes, which are data rather than costume.
        STORY: A visitor filters down the left, recognises listings by their mark, and opens one to
        find what it needs and where it came from held still on the right.
        FIRST VIEWPORT: Browse - facet rail left, search above a two-column card grid right.
        Listing - one header card, banner as its lid, icon overlapping the seam, primary action top
        right.
        FORM: Modrinth anatomy, pinned by the brief; palette, copy and voice stay this site's own.
        FINISH: unreviewed and undocumented is unfinished; this build ends with the finish review,
        the verdict, and DESIGN.md
        -->
        """);
}
